  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  Warm weather is forecast through next week.\n\nDry weather will continue across the area through early next\nweek. Surface high pressure and upper level ridging look to \nremain the dominant features across the area for the next \nseveral days. A dry cold front will pass over the region later \ntoday, but not before afternoon temperatures warm into the mid\nto upper 70s.\n\nCloudy conditions moving over the area from an upstream system\nwill lead to cooler temperatures on Saturday for Halloween and \nTrick or Treating festivities. Cooler temperatures will linger \nthrough Sunday, however these temperatures will still be above \nnormal for late Oct/early Nov.\n\nHeights build again by the middle of next week in advance of a\ndeep trough moving through the central United States. Although\nincreasing cloud cover is likely, increasing southerly flow will\noffset this, allowing temperatures to climb again to well above\nnormal through late next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending wet late next week with best chance of \nrain starting mid-week. \n\nAll models have officially backed off on any widespread\nprecipitation chances for the region early next week. High\npressure will hold strong over the Mid-Atlantic, keeping most\nrainfall chances confined to the southeast states. Cannot rule\nout a few stray showers given increased southerly flow, but\noverall the best forcing and moisture will remain well to the\nsouth.\n\nGuidance remains in agreement that a deep upper trough will dig\nsouth into the Gulf states by midweek, then move over the mid-\nAtlantic and into New England by late week. There also appears \nto be strong support for a much more favorable environment for \nmoisture to be pulled north ahead of this aforementioned upper \nlevel trough into the region from the Gulf and Atlantic. While \nit is still too soon to discuss specifics of this system, it \nwill likely be the next system to provide any meaningful \nwidespread precipitation to the region. Timing and specifics on \nthis system are still in question.",
